@using ListingStudio.Web.Components.Articles

<p class="article-lede">
    A single walkthrough video asks one clip to do every job: stop the scroll, show the home, explain the price and get a
    buyer to reach out. Short-form feeds rarely reward that. This guide explains how Creative Listing Studio reads a listing's
    verified facts and media, recommends the vertical videos that listing can support, and makes each one for a
    different purpose: reach, engagement or buyer interest.
</p>

<h2 id="one-walkthrough">Why one generic walkthrough isn't enough</h2>
<p>
    A viewer decides in the first second or two whether to keep watching, and different openings win different viewers.
    A striking kitchen earns a pause from people who aren't shopping yet. A clear price and location speaks to people who
    are. An agent on camera builds trust with buyers who want a guide. One walkthrough can't open all three ways at once,
    so it usually opens none of them well.
</p>
<p>
    Public examples point the same way. In the documented real estate TikToks we reviewed, the videos with the most views
    were curiosity and personality videos, while plain walkthroughs with a price hook, such as an agency's "what
    £750,000 buys" tour, earned roughly 12% to 23% likes per view
    (<a href="@Sources.Negotiator.Url" rel="noopener">The Negotiator</a>,
    <a href="@Sources.EstateAgentToday.Url" rel="noopener">Estate Agent Today</a>). A typical real estate post earns about
    3.1% total engagement per view (<a href="@Sources.DashSocial.Url" rel="noopener">Dash Social, H1 2025</a>).
</p>
<p class="article-note" data-testid="directional-note">
    Treat these numbers as directional research, not a forecast or a guarantee. They come from a handful of widely
    reported videos, and videos that do well get written about while the rest don't. Your own results depend on the home,
    the market, your audience and the account you post from.
</p>

<h2 id="how-it-chooses">How the recommendation engine chooses formats</h2>
<p>
    When you open a campaign, Creative Listing Studio scores every format against the listing you have, using fixed, published
    rules rather than guesswork:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Verified facts:</strong> the price, beds and baths, square footage, city and state you entered.</li>
    <li><strong>Analyzed media:</strong> which rooms your photos show, how strong and how sharp each one is, and how much processed video you have.</li>
    <li><strong>Inputs you confirmed:</strong> an approved voiceover or room-by-room narration, a story you confirmed, presenter consent, and any price change or access timing you stated.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Each format gets a score out of 100 with plain-language reasons and a list of what's missing. The three strongest
    formats the listing supports are recommended for this listing, and they are chosen so the set covers different
    purposes rather than three versions of the same angle. You can keep the recommendation, pick your own formats, or make
    every eligible video.
</p>

<h2 id="four-formats">The four first-release formats</h2>
<div class="article-table-wrap">
    <table class="article-table">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">The four first-release formats</caption>
        <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Format</th><th scope="col">Designed for</th><th scope="col">How it opens</th><th scope="col">What it needs</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <th scope="row">Wow feature</th>
                <td>Reach new viewers</td>
                <td>Leads with the home's standout room or outdoor space, then moves through the other feature areas.</td>
                <td>A standout photo of a main room or outdoor space and at least four usable storyboard photos.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <th scope="row">Price first</th>
                <td>Help buyers compare</td>
                <td>Opens with the price, the place and the basics, for example "What $450,000 gets you in Raleigh, NC".</td>
                <td>The listing price, beds and baths (or lot size for land), city and state, and at least three usable photos.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <th scope="row">Hosted POV</th>
                <td>Bring in inquiries</td>
                <td>You walk the viewer through the home, on camera or in your own words.</td>
                <td>Host footage, your approved narration, or a presenter portrait you have consented to use.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <th scope="row">Story reveal</th>
                <td>Keep viewers watching</td>
                <td>Sets up the problem the home solved and pays it off with the result.</td>
                <td>A story you confirmed (the problem and the payoff), ideally with before and after photos.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    An optional fifth format, <strong>Exclusive access</strong>, appears only when you have confirmed dated access or timing
    language, such as a private preview, and there is enough property video for an inside look.
</p>

<h2 id="inputs-fallbacks">Required inputs and what happens without them</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Missing inputs are listed, not guessed.</strong> If a format can't be made, you see exactly what it needs, such as "The listing price" or "Approved narration".</li>
    <li><strong>Optional inputs make a format stronger.</strong> Square footage strengthens the price card, and before and after photos strengthen a story.</li>
    <li><strong>Photos still being analyzed:</strong> formats that depend on a standout photo wait for the analysis rather than picking one blindly.</li>
    <li><strong>If no format qualifies,</strong> or none of the chosen videos can be rendered, the campaign falls back to the single shared vertical video, so you always get a vertical video to post.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="verification">Every claim comes from something verified</h2>
<p>
    Hooks, captions and narration only use facts you entered, stories you approved and inputs you confirmed. Creative
    Listing Studio never infers a renovation, exclusivity, a neighborhood claim, affordability, a price change or anything about
    the seller. If the listing text or an AI-written story mentions a renovation or early access that you haven't
    confirmed, that format stays unavailable and the reason tells you why. A presenter likeness is used only with
    consent on file.
</p>

<h2 id="review">Review before you publish</h2>
<p>
    Every video in the library is reviewed on its own. Watch each one, approve it or ask for changes in plain words, and
    download it as a 9:16 file for TikTok, Instagram Reels or YouTube Shorts, or download the whole library at once.
    Nothing is posted for you: you choose what to publish, where and when, and you can check your MLS and state rules
    before you do.
</p>

<h2 id="metrics">The numbers to watch after you post</h2>
<p>Views and likes tell you about reach. Leads and showings tell you whether the video is working for the listing. Track both:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Reach and engagement:</strong> views, like rate, shares, saves and how many viewers watched to the end.</li>
    <li><strong>Business results:</strong> link clicks, leads and booked showings.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Creative Listing Studio lets you add each post's link and copy in the numbers from the platform's analytics, by hand or as a
    CSV, and compares the videos from the same listing side by side. A video with fewer likes but more qualified leads
    stays marked as the business winner. Give each post a few days before comparing, and compare videos of the same
    listing rather than across very different homes.
</p>

<aside class="article-cta">
    <div>
        <h2>Turn one listing into the vertical videos most likely to earn attention</h2>
        <p>Creative Listing Studio analyzes your property's strongest visuals, verified facts and approved stories, then recommends ready-to-post creative angles.</p>
    </div>
    <MudButton Href="@CtaHref" Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" Size="Size.Large" Class="cta-on-night"
               EndIcon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ArrowForward" data-testid="article-cta">Start creating</MudButton>
</aside>

<section class="article-sources" aria-labelledby="sources">
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ol>
        @foreach (var source in Sources.All)
        {
            <li><a href="@source.Url" rel="noopener">@source.Title</a>, @source.Publisher, @source.Date.</li>
        }
    </ol>
</section>

@code {
    public const string Slug = "one-listing-four-tiktoks";

    /// <summary>The CTA goes through the click counter (placement only, no property details) before registration.</summary>
    private static string CtaHref => ArticleLinks.Track(Slug, ArticleLinks.Placements.ArticleCta, "/Account/Register");

    private sealed record Source(string Title, string Publisher, string Date, string Url);

    private static class Sources
    {
        public static readonly Source Negotiator = new(
            "Is this the first property listing to go viral on TikTok?", "The Negotiator", "2022",
            "https://thenegotiator.co.uk/news/is-this-the-first-property-listing-to-go-viral-on-tiktok/");

        public static readonly Source EstateAgentToday = new(
            "Agent explains why TikTok beats Rightmove for views", "Estate Agent Today", "March 2023",
            "https://www.estateagenttoday.co.uk/breaking-news/2023/03/agent-explains-why-tiktok-beats-rightmove-views/");

        public static readonly Source DashSocial = new(
            "Real Estate Industry Benchmark, H1 2025", "Dash Social", "2025",
            "https://pages.dashsocial.com/hubfs/social-media-benchmark/2025/1H/real-estate-industry.pdf");

        public static readonly Source[] All = [Negotiator, EstateAgentToday, DashSocial];
    }
}
